{# What the `filters` aside has narrowed the list to, stated once ABOVE THE ROWS
   (C22). A column of eight controls is where the narrowing is done; it does not
   answer "what am I looking at" at a glance, so the answer is written here,
   immediately above the results it describes. It renders in `canvas.main`, above
   the results — which is where `facet_sidebar` already put it before the layout
   moved into `canvas` (BB-378 D15).

   THE CHIPS WRAP IN A ROW, and the position indicator sits beneath them,
   left-aligned. Both follow from where this renders. The third iteration put it
   inside a 236px aside, where a row had nowhere to go and the chips had to be a
   column; the fourth moved it back into the main region, which is as wide as
   the table, so a column of chips would push the rows down for nothing. What is
   NOT restored is the spacer that used to shove the indicator to the far right:
   the human asked for this left-aligned, and a value flung to the opposite edge
   of a wide region is read last rather than read with the chips.

   The horizontal padding matches the table's cells (`px-[10px]`) so the first
   chip starts on the same vertical line as the first column heading. Aligning it
   to the region instead would be a second, nearly-right edge two pixels off the
   one below it, which reads as a mistake rather than as a decision.

   THE OPERATOR IS DERIVED FROM THE NUMBER OF VALUES, not typed by the caller:
   one value reads "is", several read "is either of". That is Linear's own
   behaviour, and deriving it is what keeps it right — a scene that had to type
   the operator per facet would eventually type the wrong one. A facet may
   override it (`includes any of` for labels, `is not` for a negation), and then
   the override is that facet's own word.

   THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO GIVE THIS COMPONENT ITS CHIPS, and the second one is
   new. `facets` is the props route: a caller that holds the applied set as data
   hands it over and this template writes the sentences. `chips` is the SLOT
   route: a caller that can only hand over ALREADY-RENDERED chips — a Views area
   whose markup another component produced — puts them there instead. The slot
   wins when both are given, because a caller that filled it has markup this
   template cannot second-guess.

   THE SLOT EXISTS FOR ONE MEASURED REASON: `empty_label` was unreachable
   without it. `views_filters_summary`'s area is declared `empty: true`, so
   `Result::render()` returns `['#markup' => '']` when no filter has a value and
   Views' `header` is TRUTHY either way — there is no Views option, no config
   and no condition that distinguishes "the module stated nothing" from "the
   module was never asked". A component CAN tell, because a slot that renders
   nothing renders nothing: `chips|render|striptags|trim` is the whole test. So
   the caller nests one of these inside another — the inner one in the
   `chips_only` variant, writing sentences from `facets`; the outer one owning
   the wrapper, the accessible name and the unfiltered statement — and the
   outer states `empty_label` exactly when the inner produced no text.

   `striptags` RATHER THAN `trim` ALONE, and both halves of that were measured
   rather than reasoned. On the tickets surface `trim` would do: the module's
   strip arrives as a plain-string `#markup`, so `Xss::filterAdmin()` has
   already removed every HTML comment before this template sees it — measured,
   `filterAdmin("<!-- THEME DEBUG -->\n<span>x</span>")` returns
   `"\n<span>x</span>"`. But the slot is a CONTRACT and not that one surface: a
   caller that fills it from an ordinary Drupal render with
   `twig.config.debug` on hands over `<!-- THEME DEBUG -->` comments, which
   `trim` keeps and which would make an empty slot read as full on exactly the
   surfaces that check the most. `striptags` drops comments and tags alike and
   leaves the text, which is what "did this state anything" means. The cost is
   stated too: a slot whose only content is an image states nothing by this
   test. Both filters exist in PHP Twig and in Storybook's Twing environment
   (`render` through `@christianwiedemann/drupal-twig-extensions`).

   THE `chips_only` VARIANT IS THE INNER HALF, and it renders NO WRAPPER: no
   root element, no border, no padding, no meta. Two reasons, both load-bearing.
   A wrapper would double the border and the padding the outer already draws.
   And an always-present wrapper would make the emptiness test above always
   false — the inner would render a div even with nothing to say, and the outer
   would never reach `empty_label`, which is the defect this variant exists to
   fix. With no facets it emits nothing at all.

   A CHIP IS REMOVABLE, AND THE AFFORDANCE IS A LINK. This template used to say
   the opposite — "a chip is a statement, not a control", "nothing here posts,
   and a little × that removed nothing would be a worse lie than no × at all" —
   and it was right for as long as this screen had no query. It has one now, so
   the caller knows the address the list was narrowed by and can hand each facet
   the address MINUS that facet; following it is a real removal. The reason it is
   an ANCHOR and not a button is the same reason it is not a script: the region
   this renders inside is boosted, so following an anchor swaps that region's
   fragment and pushes the shorter URL exactly as picking a facet does, and it
   still works with JavaScript off. Nothing here posts, which is the half that
   never changed. (The boost declaration itself is deliberately not named here —
   no component may carry one, and naming the attribute in a comment is the
   invitation that rule exists to refuse.)

   THE × IS A TEXT GLYPH AND NOT THE SPRITE ICON, which is a correction of what
   this file used to draw. It included `bb_theme:icon` with `x-circle`, and on
   the one surface that matters the icon never arrived: `views_filters_summary`
   returns its strip as a plain-string `#markup`, so
   `Renderer::ensureMarkupIsSafe()` puts everything below through
   `Xss::filterAdmin()`, whose tag list holds neither `svg` nor `use`. The
   `<svg><use>` was deleted and the anchor arrived with NO CONTENT — an
   `inline-flex` element with no content has a zero box, so the affordance was
   invisible and unclickable. A stylesheet rule used to put a `×` back through
   `::after`; that rule is gone, because the honest place for the glyph is here
   and a text `×` is something no XSS filter has a reason to remove.

   THE ACCESSIBLE NAME CARRIES NO COLON, for the other half of the same seam.
   It read `Remove filter: {{ field }}`, and `Xss::filterAdmin()` also
   protocol-filters attribute values: `filterBadProtocol()` treats everything up
   to the first colon as a scheme and drops it, so the name arrived as
   `" State"`. `Remove the State filter` says the same thing with the words in
   an order that has no colon in it.

   IT REMOVES THE WHOLE FACET, NOT ONE VALUE. A chip is one facet and its
   sentence is about the facet, so one control that undoes one statement is the
   honest granularity — and the accessible name says which, because an × whose
   scope a reader has to guess is the lie the old comment was worried about.
   Removing a single value belongs to the `filters` aside, where every option row
   is already a real control. A facet with no `remove_url` renders no × at all.

   Emptiness is tested with `is not empty` rather than bare truthiness, because
   this template is rendered by PHP Twig in Drupal and by Twing in Storybook, and
   the two disagree about `[]` — PHP reads it as false, JavaScript as true. The
   guard has to hold on both, which is the defect link-group records in full. #}
{%- set bb_facets = facets|default([]) -%}
{%- set bb_slot = chips is defined and chips is not empty ? chips|render|striptags|trim : '' -%}
{%- set bb_own %}
  {%- for facet in bb_facets %}
    {%- set values = facet.values|default([]) %}
    {%- set derived = values|length > 1 ? 'is either of' : 'is' %}
    <span data-facet class="inline-flex items-baseline flex-wrap gap-x-1 px-2 py-1 rounded-xs bg-primary/10 border border-transparent text-meta text-primary">
      <span data-part="field" class="font-semibold">{{ facet.field }}</span>
      <span data-part="operator" class="text-text-faint">{{ facet.operator|default(derived) }}</span>
      <span data-part="values">{{ values|join(', ') }}</span>
      {%- if facet.remove_url|default('') is not empty %}
        <a data-remove-facet class="inline-flex self-center text-body leading-none text-text-faint no-underline transition-colors duration-fast hover:text-text focus-visible:text-text focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring"
          href="{{ facet.remove_url }}"
          aria-label="Remove the {{ facet.field }} filter"
          >×</a>
      {%- endif %}
    </span>
  {%- endfor %}
{% endset -%}
{%- if variant|default('') == 'chips_only' -%}
  {{- bb_facets is not empty ? bb_own -}}
{%- else -%}
<div{{ (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass([
    'flex', 'flex-col', 'items-start', 'gap-2',
    'px-3', 'pb-4',
  ]) }}
  {%- if label %} aria-label="{{ label }}"{% endif %}>
  <div data-active-filters="chips" class="flex flex-row flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-2">
    {%- if bb_slot is not empty %}
      {% block chips %}{{ chips }}{% endblock %}
    {%- elseif bb_facets is not empty %}
      {{- bb_own -}}
    {%- else %}
      <span data-active-filters="empty" class="text-meta text-text-light">{{ empty_label|default('All results') }}</span>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
  {%- if meta %}
    <span data-active-filters="meta">{% block meta %}{{ meta }}{% endblock %}</span>
  {%- endif %}
</div>
{%- endif -%}
